DEFLATE(3)                     P32W Unix                      DEFLATE(3)

NAME
       deflate, deflate_raw, inflate_raw, inflate_rest - Deflate codec

SYNOPSIS
       #include <deflate.h>

       typedef int (*zin_fn)(void *ctx);
       typedef int (*zread_fn)(void *ctx, unsigned char *p,
                               unsigned n);
       typedef int (*zout_fn)(void *ctx, const unsigned char *p,
                              unsigned n);

       int deflate_raw(zread_fn read, void *ictx, zout_fn out,
                       void *octx, int level);
       int inflate_raw(zin_fn in, void *ictx, zout_fn out,
                       void *octx);
       int inflate_rest(unsigned char *rest);

DESCRIPTION
       These compress and expand data in the Deflate format of RFC
       1951, the one inside gzip and zip files (gzip(1), zip(1),
       unzip(1) use them). They read raw Deflate data only: the gzip
       or zip headers and trailers are the caller's business (with
       crc32(3) for their checksums). Data streams through
       functions the caller supplies, so neither side needs a whole
       file in memory; ictx and octx are passed to them unchanged.

       deflate_raw compresses: it calls read(ictx, p, n) for up to n
       more bytes of input, which returns the count, 0 at the end,
       or -1 on an error; and out(octx, p, n) with n bytes of
       compressed output, which returns 0, or -1 to stop. level runs
       from 0 (no compression: stored blocks of up to 65535 bytes)
       through 1 (fastest) to 9 (smallest output, slowest); 6 is
       the usual, and a level outside 0 to 9 means 6. The method is
       zlib's: a 32 KiB window, hash chains, lazy matching, and
       blocks of up to 16384 symbols, each sent stored or with fixed
       or dynamic Huffman codes, whichever is shortest.

       inflate_raw expands: it calls in(ictx) for each byte of
       compressed input, which returns the byte (0 to 255) or -1 at
       the end, and out(octx, p, n) with the output, in pieces of
       up to 32 KiB. It stops at the end of the final block. To do
       so it may have read up to 4 bytes beyond it; inflate_rest,
       called after inflate_raw, copies those bytes to rest and
       returns how many there are, so that a caller reading a gzip
       trailer (or the next zip entry) can take them first.

RETURN VALUE
       deflate_raw and inflate_raw return 0, or one of the errors
       defined in deflate.h:

       Z_DATA (-1)   the compressed data is damaged
       Z_EOF  (-2)   the compressed data ends too soon
       Z_IO   (-3)   read, in or out reported an error

EXAMPLES
       static int rd(void *c, unsigned char *p, unsigned n)
       {
           return read(0, p, n);
       }
       static int wr(void *c, const unsigned char *p, unsigned n)
       {
           return write(1, p, n) == n ? 0 : -1;
       }
       static int in(void *f) { return getc((FILE *)f); }
       ...
       r = deflate_raw(rd, 0, wr, 0, 6);       /* stdin to stdout */
       r = inflate_raw(in, stdin, wr, 0);      /* and back */

SEE ALSO
       gzip(1), zip(1), unzip(1), tar(1), crc32(3)

BUGS
       The state is in static variables: one compression and one
       expansion can run at a time, not two of either. Calling
       deflate_raw adds about 200 KB of static buffers to a
       program's bss, and inflate_raw about 40 KB.
       Z_MEM (-4) is defined but never returned: no memory is
       allocated. There is no zlib-format (RFC 1950) or gzip-format
       interface, no preset dictionary, and no way to flush part of
       a stream.
